    <DataNode elementId="AT1G08830-MONOMER" textLabel="Superoxide dismutase [Cu-Zn] 1" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="P24704" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="750" centerY="635" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">NIL The cDNA encoding the copper/zinc-dependent superoxide dismutase (SOD) CSD1 has been isolated from &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [1731963]|. CSD1 belongs to a small gene family in Arabidopsis with at least three copper/zinc-dependent SOD&apos;s. Its molecular function (Cu/Zn-dependent SOD) could be confirmed in a non-denaturative PAGE assay and assigned to CSD1 by immunodetection of the corresponding antiserum. The CSD1 gene was expressed in rosette leaves under standard 16-h-photoperiod growth conditions to about 25% compared to CSD2 but increased upon ozone fumigation, UV-B exposure |CITS: [ 9765550]| and salicylic acid analogs |CITS: [10550898]|, indicating that CSD1 could be a general stress-response enzyme. It has been demonstrated that a copper-chaperone (AtCCS, At1g12520) is crucial for the activation of all three Cu/Zn-dependent SOD&apos;s in Arabdiopsis (Chu et al. 2005 - 16126858).</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G08830-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT1G08830.1" />
